From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ivo Nunes <ivo@nunes.uk>
Date: Sun, 15 Nov 2026 23:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Rendering: add the Parametric tonemapper and make it the
 default

Godot's default is Linear, which clips anything brighter than white, and
its film-like options either need a white value tuned per scene (Filmic,
ACES) or lift the shadows and mute colour (AgX). Parametric grades in the
ACEScg (AP1) colour space with ACES's desaturation either side of a
curve that keeps mid grey at 0.18, has a straight middle (slope), an
exponential toe and shoulder that meet it with the same slope, and a
white clip so the brightest light reaches white. The four controls are
Environment.tonemap_parametric_slope, _toe, _shoulder and _white_clip.

Environment resources and the rendering server now default to it, and
the 3D editor's preview environment uses it when tonemapping is on.
Its white for glow's screen blend mode is the scene value where the
shoulder reaches 1.0 (about 18 with the defaults).

Forward+ and Mobile only: Purr builds without the Compatibility
renderer.

Test: render a scene with sky, sun, glow and emissive surfaces at 2, 8
and 32 energy on Forward+ and Mobile with each tonemapper; Parametric
keeps mid grey, highlights roll off to white without hue shifts and
glow doesn't darken bright pixels. A new Environment reports
tonemap_mode Parametric.

Upstream: Purr only (changes Godot's default).
